### The Fastest Request is One That Doesn't Happen

Caching stores the result of an expensive operation so it can be served quickly on subsequent requests.

#### Cache-Aside (Lazy Loading) — Most Common

```js
async function getUser(userId) {
  // 1. Check cache first
  const cached = await redis.get(`user:${userId}`);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached);

  // 2. Cache miss: fetch from database
  const user = await db.users.findById(userId);
  if (!user) return null;

  // 3. Store in cache for next time (TTL: 5 minutes)
  await redis.setex(`user:${userId}`, 300, JSON.stringify(user));
  return user;
}

// On update: invalidate the cache
async function updateUser(userId, data) {
  const user = await db.users.update(userId, data);
  await redis.del(`user:${userId}`); // Force fresh fetch next time
  return user;
}
```

#### Cache Invalidation Strategies

| Strategy | How it works | Best for |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **TTL (Time-to-live)** | Cache expires after N seconds | Data that changes infrequently |
| **Event-based** | Invalidate on write (as above) | Data that must be fresh |
| **Cache-aside** | App manages read/write explicitly | Most use cases |
| **Write-through** | Write to cache AND DB simultaneously | High-read, low-write |

#### What to Cache

- ✅ Database query results (user profiles, product catalogs)
- ✅ Computed results (leaderboards, analytics summaries)
- ✅ External API responses
- ❌ User-specific sensitive data (or use per-user cache keys)
- ❌ Data that must always be current (stock prices, payments)

