### Decoupling Services with Async Messaging

A message queue lets services communicate without being directly connected. The producer publishes a message; the consumer processes it at its own pace.

```
Without queue:
User registers → API must send email NOW → if email service is slow, user waits

With queue:
User registers → API publishes 'user.registered' event → returns 201 immediately
                                              ↓
                              Email service picks it up async → sends email
```

#### BullMQ (Redis-based Queue for Node.js)

```js
import { Queue, Worker } from 'bullmq';

// Producer: add jobs to queue
const emailQueue = new Queue('emails', { connection: redisClient });

app.post('/api/register', async (req, res) => {
  const user = await db.users.create(req.body);

  // Fire and forget — no await needed
  await emailQueue.add('welcome-email', {
    to: user.email,
    name: user.name
  });

  res.status(201).json(user);
});

// Consumer: process jobs (can run on a separate server)
const worker = new Worker('emails', async (job) => {
  const { to, name } = job.data;
  await sendEmail({
    to,
    subject: 'Welcome to Vertex!',
    html: `<h1>Hi ${name}, welcome aboard!</h1>`
  });
}, { connection: redisClient });

worker.on('completed', job => console.log(`Email sent: ${job.id}`));
worker.on('failed', (job, err) => console.error(`Email failed: ${err.message}`));
```

#### Use Cases for Message Queues

- Sending emails/notifications
- Processing image/video uploads
- Generating reports
- Syncing data to analytics systems
- Any slow operation that doesn't need an immediate response

